---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image-to-500kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={500} inputLabel="Drop the photos that need to fit a 500 KB limit" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>500 KB: small enough to send, big enough to look right</h2>
    <p>
      At half a megabyte, most 12-megapixel phone photos get a quality of 82–90, which is above the
      point where compression artefacts become visible at normal viewing size. This makes 500 KB the
      practical limit for files you send to other people: email attachments, forum and marketplace
      listings, insurance claims, estate agent uploads and support tickets.
    </p>

    <h2>Email attachment arithmetic</h2>
    <p>
      Mail servers apply their limit to the encoded message, not to your files. Attachments are
      base64-encoded in transit, which adds about 33%, plus the message body and headers. A “25 MB
      limit” is thus about 18 MB of real files:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mail limit</th><th>Usable payload</th><th>Photos at 500 KB</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10 MB (many corporate gateways)</td><td>≈ 7.5 MB</td><td>15</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20 MB (Outlook / Microsoft 365)</td><td>≈ 15 MB</td><td>30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25 MB (Gmail)</td><td>≈ 18 MB</td><td>36</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50 MB</td><td>≈ 37 MB</td><td>74</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The limit of the recipient also applies, and it is usually the smaller of the two. If you are not
      sure, split a large set across two messages so that none bounces.
    </p>

    <h2>Will 500 KB hurt the photo?</h2>
    <p>
      Seldom at normal sizes. The damage shows in three places: smooth gradients such as a clear sky
      can get faint banding, hard high-contrast edges can get a thin halo, and fine repeating texture
      (fabric weave, foliage, gravel) softens first. If your image is mostly one of these, expand the
      search table in the result and check the quality the tool chose. Above 85, there is no problem.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping full resolution</h2>
    <p>
      At a 500 KB target, the quality search almost always succeeds alone, so the pixel dimensions do
      not change. To guarantee this, turn off “Shrink if quality is not enough”. You then get the
      smallest file the quality search can make, with a warning if it missed the target. This matters
      for insurance and claims photos, where cropping or resizing can weaken the evidence.
    </p>

    <h2>Sending many photos at once</h2>
    <p>
      Drop in the whole set. Each photo gets its own search to 500 KB, and “Download all as .zip” makes
      one archive to attach. This also stops mail clients from compressing or rotating your images
      again. There is no limit on the number of photos, because the work runs on your machine.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy matters for this size of file too</h2>
    <p>
      Files of 500 KB are often holiday photos, house interiors, damaged vehicles, injuries and
      documents. Nothing here is uploaded: the decode, the search and the encode run in this tab. The
      exported file also has no EXIF data, so it does not contain the GPS coordinates of your home.
    </p>

    <h2>Other targets</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb">200 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb">1 MB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb">100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
